    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Painting a ceiling is not harder than painting a wall; it is less forgiving. Light rakes across it from every window, gravity works against you the whole time, and the flaws you leave are the ones you will stare at from the bed or the sofa for the next decade. The good news is that the method is simple, and once you know why each step exists, a ceiling becomes a two-hour job rather than a weekend of regret.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Prep the Room Before You Open a Can
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by getting as much out of the room as you can. Anything left behind will be under a ceiling that drips, and even the best low-spatter ceiling paint throws a fine mist off the roller when you push it quickly. Furniture that cannot leave should go to the middle of the room in one tight cluster, covered with plastic sheeting and then a canvas drop cloth on top. Plastic alone is slippery underfoot and lets drips run; canvas alone lets wet paint soak through to whatever is beneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cover the floor wall to wall. Canvas drop cloths are worth their price because they stay put, absorb drips instead of letting you track them, and can be reused for years. Overlap the seams by a foot and push the edges tight to the baseboard so a drip landing at the wall has somewhere to go other than the carpet. If you are painting over carpet, a self-adhesive carpet film under the canvas is cheap insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn off the power to ceiling fixtures at the breaker, then remove what you can. Light fixtures usually drop down on two screws; let them hang by their wires inside a plastic bag or, better, disconnect them if you are comfortable doing so. Ceiling fans can stay in place with their blades removed and the motor housing bagged. Smoke detector bases and vent covers come off with a screwdriver, and painting around them is always worse than painting where they were.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wash the ceiling if it has any history. Kitchen ceilings carry a film of cooking grease, bathroom ceilings carry soap residue and sometimes mildew, and ceilings above a fireplace or in a former smoker's home carry soot and nicotine. Paint will go on over all of it, but it will not hold well and stains will bleed through. A sponge mop with a mild degreaser, followed by a clean-water rinse and a full day to dry, solves most of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, light the room properly. Ceilings look flawless under a single overhead bulb and terrible under daylight from a window, so set up a work light on the floor aimed at a low angle across the ceiling. That raking light shows you the holidays, missed spots and lap marks while the paint is still wet enough to fix, which is the only time fixing them is easy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Prep is most of the job.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you budget three hours for a ceiling, expect two of them to be emptying the room, covering, removing fixtures, washing and patching. The rolling is the quick part.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Tools That Actually Make It Easier
          </h2>

          <p>
            An extension pole is the single most useful tool for a ceiling, and it matters more than the brand of paint. Working from the floor with a pole keeps your head out of the spatter zone, lets you apply even pressure across a long stroke and saves you from moving a ladder every two minutes. A pole that extends from roughly four to eight feet suits standard eight- and nine-foot ceilings; vaulted ceilings need a longer one and more patience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose the roller cover for the surface, not the price. For smooth drywall ceilings, a 3/8-inch nap is the common choice and the one our ceiling paint guide is built around: it holds enough paint to cover well without leaving a heavy stipple. Lightly textured ceilings take a 1/2-inch nap so the fibers reach into the texture. Heavily textured or popcorn ceilings need a thicker nap still, or a sprayer, and a gentle touch because the texture can come loose when it gets wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good angled sash brush, around two to two and a half inches, handles cutting in. The difference between a cheap brush and a good one shows up most at the ceiling line, where a brush that holds a sharp edge saves you from taping. Our <a href="/best-paint-brushes/">paint brush picks</a> cover what to look for in the bristles and ferrule. A sturdy step ladder for cutting in, a roller frame with a threaded handle, and a five-gallon bucket with a roller grid round out the kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A bucket and grid beat a roller tray on a ceiling job. A tray holds little paint, tips easily and has to be refilled every few minutes; a bucket with a grid holds a gallon or more, lets you load the roller evenly and sits stable on the floor while you work from a pole. If you are painting more than one room, it is worth having a second grid so you can keep a bucket of paint fresh while you clean the first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Painter's tape is optional. If you are painting the walls a different color after the ceiling, you can skip tape entirely and simply cut the ceiling slightly down onto the wall, then cut the wall color back up later. If the walls are already finished and staying, low-tack tape on the wall at the ceiling line protects them. Our <a href="/best-painters-tape/">painter's tape guide</a> covers which tapes release cleanly from freshly painted walls.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ceiling surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Roller nap</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smooth drywall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common default; low stipple, good hide</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light orange peel or knockdown</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reaches into texture without heavy stipple</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy texture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Load generously, roll gently</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Popcorn</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick nap or sprayer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Test a patch; texture can soften and fall</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="patch-prime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Patch Cracks and Prime What Needs It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look over the whole ceiling with your raking light before any paint goes up. Nail pops, hairline cracks at drywall seams, old anchor holes and dents all show more on a fresh flat finish than they did on the old dull one. Small holes and dents take a lightweight spackle; longer seam cracks are better handled with joint compound and, if they keep reopening, a strip of mesh tape. Our comparison of <a href="/joint-compound-vs-spackle/">joint compound and spackle</a> explains which one suits which repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sand patches flush once they are dry, then wipe the dust away with a damp cloth. Any ridge you leave at the edge of a patch will show through the paint as a halo, especially near a window. Feather the edges of larger repairs a few inches beyond the patch so the transition disappears. Our <a href="/best-spackle/">spackle picks</a> include the lightweight products that shrink least and sand most easily overhead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spot-prime every patch. Fresh spackle and joint compound are far more absorbent than the painted ceiling around them, so they soak up the binder from the topcoat and dry with a different sheen. This is called flashing, and it is the most common reason a carefully patched ceiling still shows its repairs. A coat of primer over each patch seals it so the topcoat dries evenly across the whole surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some ceilings need a full coat of primer rather than spot priming. Water stains, smoke or nicotine discoloration and old grease bleed through ordinary latex paint, sometimes after it has dried and looked fine for a day. A stain-blocking primer, usually a shellac-based or oil-based product, locks those in. New, unpainted drywall also needs a full coat of drywall primer before the finish. Our <a href="/best-primer/">primer guide</a> covers which formula suits which problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the ceiling was last painted with a glossy or oil-based finish, which is common in older kitchens and bathrooms, latex paint may not grip it well. A quick test is to rub a small area with a cotton ball dampened with rubbing alcohol; if paint comes off on the cotton, it is latex, and if it does not, it is likely oil. Oil-based or glossy ceilings need a light scuff sanding and a bonding primer before you roll the topcoat.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WATCH FOR</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Stains that come back.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a water stain reappears through a coat of ordinary paint, more paint will not fix it. Let the ceiling dry, spot it with a stain-blocking primer, then repaint. And make sure the leak that caused it is actually fixed first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cut-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cut In the Edges First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cutting in means brushing a band of paint along every edge the roller cannot reach: where the ceiling meets the walls, around fixture boxes, around vents and along any beams or soffits. Do it before you roll so the roller can overlap onto the brushed band and blend the two textures together. The band should be about two to three inches wide, enough that the roller never has to come right up to the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in sections rather than cutting in the whole room at once. If you brush the entire perimeter and then start rolling, the first edges you cut will be dry by the time the roller reaches them, and the brushed band will show as a different texture and sheen along the walls. Cutting in one wall, rolling that section, then moving on keeps the brushed band wet when the roller meets it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Load the brush about a third of the way up the bristles and tap off the excess against the inside of the can rather than wiping it on the rim. Start a little away from the wall, then work the brush toward the corner with the angled tip leading, letting the bristles fan into the joint. A steady hand and a good brush will give a straighter line than tape in most rooms, and it is far quicker once you are comfortable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a light pass with the brush across the band before moving on, feathering the inner edge so it does not leave a heavy ridge. A thick band of paint along the ceiling edge dries more slowly and with a slightly different sheen than the rolled area, and in strong side light it shows as a frame around the room. Thin and feathered is what you want.
          </p>

          <p>
            Around light fixture boxes and vents, cut in neatly and then let the roller handle the rest. These are the spots most likely to drip, so keep the brush lightly loaded and check the area after a few minutes in case a run has formed. A drip caught wet can be brushed out in a second; one caught dry needs sanding.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rule-of-Thumb Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Width of a cut-in band</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">How far to load the brush</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4x4 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical section size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 wall</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cut in at a time</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="rolling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Roll the Field and Keep a Wet Edge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Load the roller fully, then roll it on the grid until it is evenly saturated but not dripping. An underloaded roller is the root of most ceiling problems: you end up pressing harder to make the paint go further, which leaves track marks and thin spots. A properly loaded roller should feel slightly heavy and leave a full, even film with light pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in sections about four feet square, which is the size our ceiling paint recommendations are written around. Roll the paint on in a zigzag or W pattern to distribute it, then fill in without lifting the roller, then finish with light parallel strokes all in the same direction. For the final pass, roll parallel to the main window wall, so that the slight texture left by the roller runs with the incoming light rather than across it. The <a href="/ceiling-paint-sheen/">sheen you choose</a> matters here too, because flatter finishes hide roller texture far better.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most important habit is keeping a wet edge. Each new section should overlap slightly into the previous one while that previous one is still wet, so the two films melt together. If the edge dries before you reach it, the overlap becomes a visible lap mark: a stripe of double-thick paint with a different sheen. Work steadily across the shorter dimension of the room so you return to each edge quickly, and do not stop for a break in the middle of a ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resist the urge to go back into a section that has started to set. Flat ceiling paint begins to skin over within a few minutes, and rolling into it then drags and tears the film, leaving a rough patch that shows clearly once dry. If you see a thin spot after the paint has started to set, leave it and catch it on the second coat. Our <a href="/ceiling-paint-roller-marks/">guide to ceiling roller marks</a> covers what causes flashing and lap marks and how to fix them if they appear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilation and temperature affect how long your wet edge lasts. In a hot, dry room with a fan blowing, paint sets faster and lap marks become much more likely. Close windows near the work area while you roll, turn off ceiling fans and forced-air vents blowing across the ceiling, and if the weather is hot, paint in the cooler part of the day. Open everything up again once the ceiling is finished.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Finish every section in one direction.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">However you spread the paint, make the last light pass in each section parallel to the main window wall. The ceiling will look smoother in daylight, and lap marks are less visible when every stroke runs the same way.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="second-coat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Decide Whether You Need a Second Coat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most ceilings need two coats, and it is usually better to plan for two thin coats than to try to get away with one thick one. A heavy single coat sags, drips and dries unevenly, and it still tends to leave thin spots that show in side light. Two moderate coats give a more uniform color and sheen, hide minor surface imperfections better and cure harder. Budget for it when you buy: our guide to <a href="/how-much-paint-for-a-ceiling/">how much paint a ceiling needs</a> works out the gallons for two coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can sometimes get away with one coat when you are refreshing a ceiling that is already the same white, the existing paint is sound and the surface is clean. Even then, inspect it in daylight once it has dried. If you see patchy areas, roller tracks or the ghost of a patch, a second coat is the fix. Color-change ceiling paints, which go on tinted and dry white, make it easier to spot missed areas during the first coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wait for the first coat to dry to the recoat time on the label before rolling the second. For most latex ceiling paints this is a few hours, but cool or humid conditions stretch it. Rolling over paint that is dry to the touch but not ready to recoat can lift the first coat and leave a rough texture. When in doubt, wait longer, especially in a bathroom or basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Roll the second coat at right angles to the first if you can, then finish in the same direction as before, parallel to the window wall. Cutting in a second time is also worth it around the edges, because the brushed band is usually where the first coat is thinnest. Keep the same wet-edge discipline; lap marks are just as possible on the second coat as on the first.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using a paint with a stain-blocking or mildew-resistant formula for a bathroom or kitchen, follow the manufacturer's instructions on film thickness and coats. Those products only work as advertised if they are applied at the rate on the label. Our <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paint picks</a> note which paints are formulated for humid rooms and which are general-purpose.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
