        <div id="cleanup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Inspect, Touch Up and Clean Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the final coat has dried, go over the ceiling with the raking work light and then in daylight. Look for missed spots along the edges, roller tracks, drips that dried as bumps and any spots where a patch is flashing through. Small touch-ups on flat paint are usually invisible if you use the same paint, the same applicator and a light hand; large touch-ups on a ceiling almost never blend, so if an area looks wrong, repaint the whole section from edge to edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remove painter's tape while the paint is still slightly soft, pulling it back on itself at a low angle. Waiting until the paint is fully cured can tear the film along the edge and leave a ragged line. If the paint has already hardened, score the edge lightly with a utility knife before you pull the tape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean latex paint out of rollers and brushes with warm water and a little dish soap, working the paint out until the water runs clear. A roller spinner gets the water out of a roller cover quickly; otherwise squeeze it out and stand it on end to dry. Good brushes should be reshaped and hung or laid flat in their keeper so the bristles hold their shape. Oil-based primers need mineral spirits and should be disposed of according to local rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reinstall fixtures, fans and vent covers only once the paint has dried enough not to stick to them, which is usually the next day. Running a fan or replacing a fixture against fresh paint can pull the film away and leave a mark at the canopy. Restore power at the breaker after the fixtures are back in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the leftover paint. Write the room, date and product on the lid, seal it tightly and store it somewhere that does not freeze. Ceilings get scuffed less than walls, but a water stain or a fixture change a few years from now is much easier to deal with when you have the original paint on hand.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PAYOFF</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A ceiling done right lasts a decade.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ceilings are rarely touched or cleaned, so a properly prepared and painted ceiling can go many years without attention. The time you spend on prep pays you back every year after.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Wash on Kitchen and Bathroom Ceilings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grease, soap film and soot do not look like much, but paint does not bond well to them and stains bleed through. Wash with a mild degreaser, rinse with clean water and let the ceiling dry fully before you prime or paint. It is the step that decides whether the finish lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting In the Whole Room Before Rolling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brushing every edge first means the oldest edges are dry by the time the roller arrives, leaving a picture-frame band of different texture and sheen along the walls. Cut in one wall, roll that area, then move on, so the brushed band is wet when the roller blends into it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing Harder Instead of Reloading</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When the roller runs dry, the instinct is to push harder. That leaves thin, streaky patches and roller-edge tracks. Reload often, keep the roller saturated and let it do the work with light pressure. Most streaky ceilings are caused by a roller carrying too little paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going Back Into Paint That Has Started to Set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flat ceiling paint skins over within minutes. Rolling back into a section after that tears the film and leaves a rough patch that shows clearly once dry. Leave small misses alone and catch them on the second coat instead of reworking wet paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Unprimed Patches</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh spackle and joint compound soak up paint differently from the ceiling around them, so they dry with a dull halo. Spot-prime every patch before the topcoat. It takes ten minutes and saves the whole ceiling from showing its repairs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you paint the ceiling or the walls first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Paint the ceiling first. Drips and roller spatter from the ceiling inevitably land on the walls, and cutting in the ceiling slightly onto the wall is easy to cover when you paint the walls afterward. If the walls are staying as they are, tape them at the ceiling line or cut in carefully with a good angled brush.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which direction should you roll a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Spread the paint however is comfortable, but make the final light pass in each section parallel to the main source of daylight, usually the largest window wall. Roller texture that runs with the light is far less visible than texture running across it. Keep every finishing stroke in that same direction across the whole ceiling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to prime a ceiling before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. A clean, sound ceiling being repainted in a similar color usually needs only spot priming over patches. New drywall needs a drywall primer, and water stains, smoke or nicotine discoloration need a stain-blocking primer. Glossy or oil-based ceilings need a bonding primer so latex paint adheres properly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of paint does a ceiling need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most ceilings look best with two coats. One coat can be enough when you are refreshing the same white over clean, sound paint, but inspect it in daylight before deciding. Color changes, new drywall and stained ceilings all need a primer and two finish coats for an even result.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size roller nap is best for a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 3/8-inch nap is the usual choice for smooth drywall ceilings because it carries plenty of paint without leaving heavy stipple. Use a 1/2-inch nap for light texture and a thicker nap for heavy texture. Popcorn ceilings are best tested first, because the texture can loosen when wet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you avoid lap marks on a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep a wet edge by working in small sections and rolling each new section into the last while it is still wet. Close windows and turn off fans that speed drying, keep the roller fully loaded and do not stop partway across the ceiling. Finish each section with light strokes in one direction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint a ceiling without a ladder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly, yes. An extension pole lets you roll a standard eight- or nine-foot ceiling from the floor, which is safer and keeps paint out of your face. You will still want a step ladder for cutting in along the edges and around fixtures, unless you use an edging tool on a pole.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

